report:
SEAT B, independent non-author review (Opus 5.5). This is one substantive vote. I did not see any other vote or author report. I executed, hashed, imported and compiled nothing.

READ ROUTE. I read MANIFEST.json, SEGMENT-INDEX.json and all eight READ-SEGMENT files in full. Segments 06 and 07 hit the display cap, and I reread their remaining lines (06:525-640, 07:472-575) before reaching any judgment. That covers the whole bodies of the contract, API, test (566 lines), Ledger Language/Hashes/Keys, CIP-0057, SOURCES, README, adoption, tool checker, SIZES, routing, the 7 receipts, FIXTURE-STRUCTURE, the three supplements, SUPPLEMENT-SOURCES and the envelope. For the opaque B9 blueprint I read only raw lines 1-40: the preamble (plutusVersion v3, Aiken v1.1.24+bacbeb3) and the first two handlers, c2.c2.mint then c2.c2.spend. Both carry prefix 590acf, the c2 receipt hash and no parameters key. The rest of B9 is unread. I relied on FIXTURE-STRUCTURE and the test transcriptions for all 16 handlers' metadata. The original MANIFEST.json body (5602 B, 2f91b6fc…) is not in the segments and I did not read it. I treated the segments as exact transcriptions of the pinned originals but did not hash them.

ALGORITHM, DIGEST WIDTH AND TAGS (C1/C2). Every citation checks out line by line:
- Hashes:181-182 ScriptHash over ADDRHASH; Hashes:105 imports ADDRHASH; Keys:132 defines it as Hash.Blake2b_224.
- B2b:56-59 sets HashSize 28 and blake2b_libsodium 28; B2b:148-162 is unkeyed (nullPtr/0); CIP:102 says blake2b-224.
- Blake2b_256 appears only as HASH (Hashes:125) and is correctly excluded.
- Lang:199-205 hashes BS.singleton tag <> originalBytes with hashWith id. Lang:185 newtype-derives SafeToHash on Plutus; Lang:261-262 gives originalBytes = fromShort.
- Tags are V1 0x01 (:525), V2 0x02 (:545), V3 0x03 (:565), V4 0x04 (:586). The CBOR encodeEnum index (:273-278, :329-330) is used only in the envelope (:227-229). The tag trap is correctly called out.
- The [I] links are labelled honestly: the Cardano.Crypto.Hash re-export, Hash.Class hashWith/castHash, the libsodium body, and CPython equivalence.

LEDGER BYTE BOUNDARY (C3). [S] Lang:546-547 and :566-567 hash and deserialise the same PlutusBinary bs. [S at plutus HEAD] SS:265-269 decodes fromShort sScript from offset 0 with CBOR.deserialiseFromBytes; SS:263 returns sScript unmodified; SS:155-160 shows SerialisedScript is serialise . SerialiseViaFlat. So the ledger hashes tag || bs with no strip and no extra wrap, and the [enum, bytes] envelope is transport only. The remaining inferences are:
- CIP compiledCode = SerialisedScript (prose-level);
- PV2/PV3.deserialiseScript = Common.deserialiseScript;
- the outer header form;
- dependency correspondence;
- libsodium/CPython equivalence.

None of these is required for the C3 hash rule itself. The header form affects only C4 refusal. Primitive equivalence and dependency correspondence are honestly labelled and backed only by the empirical cross-check, and they do not change the rule. So I do not invoke STOP and request no missing symbol. One labelling nit is finding F2: the 'Therefore [S]' at contract line 43 inherits two [I] premises.

The supplements' HEAD commits are correctly not claimed as qualified ledger or installed-dependency correspondence (contract lines 9 and 57; API line 39). The ledger-vs-tool remainder note is accurate: SS:259-261 tolerates V1/V2 trailing bytes. C4 refuses such inputs for v2 locally; it never hashes them differently, and it makes no protocol claim.

LOCAL ADMISSION DOMAIN (C4). Admission is a local tool rule only, not maxTxSize or ExUnits:
- hex must be lowercase ASCII, at most 65536 characters, checked before decoding;
- header must be 0x58 with 24 ≤ L ≤ 255, or 0x59 with L ≥ 256;
- total length must be exactly header + L and at most 32768 bytes;
- only v2 and v3 are admitted.

The fixtures are consistent with this. CIP58ad gives 175 B = 2 + 173. The seven B9 L16 values + 3 equal SIZES appliedBytes: 0x0acf=2767, 0x32d7=13015, 0x31ed=12781, 0x1ac8=6856, 0x26d1=9937, 0x2a4d=10829, 0x2a46=10822. Hex character counts equal 2 × appliedBytes.

BLUEPRINT CHECK (C5). Covered: strict UTF-8, a duplicate-key hook (both the entry and preamble cases defeat last-wins), schema checks, remaining-parameters refusal, all 16 entries recomputed individually, and disjoint ValueError subclasses asserted in both directions (assertInputError/assertMismatch with assertNotIsInstance). 32 malformed cases and 13 bad versions are counted correctly. Gaps (non-blocking): which error wins when one entry is malformed and another mismatched is unspecified (F3), and there is no test for UTF-16/BOM input or for returning a fresh result dict (F4).

VECTORS AND FROZEN EXPECTATIONS (C6). I compared CIP57_CODE against CIP:425 in 5×70-character chunks: identical, 350 hex characters. CIP57_HASH equals CIP:426. All 7 RECEIPTS equal the hash-*.txt bodies and FIXTURE expectedHashes. The handler-to-group assignment, prefixes and lengths all match FIXTURE. Expected values are frozen literals; hashlib is used only for sha256 pins (test:209), and the tests compute no BLAKE2b. The same-run Aiken receipt limitation and the shared-primitive limitation are stated honestly. There is no authenticated ledger oracle and no formal proof of the primitive.

TEST AUDIT: exactly 19 methods. Five T00 methods need no implementation; 14 implementation methods each call _impl() or _impl_source() first:
- T01: api_surface, static_purity, cip57, b9_16, tag, cbor, length, hex, bounds, flip;
- T02: b9, cip_bounds, substitution, malformed.

While the implementation is absent, _impl_source raises AssertionError(RED). That is a unittest failure, not an error, and is never cached, so all 14 fail with the exact phrase that predictedRED expects. Module import pulls in stdlib only and reads no files, so there is no ImportError-only RED.

Loader effects: spec_from_file_location/module_from_spec/exec_module neither touch sys.path nor insert into sys.modules. -B blocks pyc writes. -I drops the script directory from sys.path and ignores PYTHON* variables but keeps MHASH_PACKET_DIR. None of the loader path runs at RED.

T00 runtime prediction from the transcribed bodies: every anchor substring is present (including the multi-line '\n' anchors at Lang:544-545, 564-565 and 274-277). The CIP slice markers are present (CIP:363, 365, 430), and the JSON parses strictly with one hello_world entry and no parameters. SOURCES statuses and shas match PINS. The tool contains '(M-HASH open)' and no 'blake2'. The adoption and README phrases are present. The B9 checks are consistent with FIXTURE and the 40 lines I read. Residual T00 risk is limited to the unread MANIFEST.json (F5) and the opaque remainder of B9. Any T00 failure or error means STOP.

The 15 meaningfulness controls are sound against the tests. MC10 depends on handler order: B9 confirms c2.c2.spend is not first; the vs4 position is unverified (F6). The static AST guard is not a sandbox and is trivially bypassable through attribute chains (F1). That does not matter at RED; it matters before any implementation is executed.

PURITY (C7) AND NON-CLAIMS (C8). C7 is accurate as worded, since it already says 'guard, not a sandbox'. C8's non-claims and open scope are complete. All 20 UTxO families, general financial obligations, ACTUS, DeFi, recursion, PCD, private handoff, multi-parent composition, residuals, X1/PX04/M-EU, FX1, FV-01/FV-02 and real Preprod stay open. Native authority is 0.

ENVELOPE. The argv matches the requested envelope exactly:
- timeout 10s with --kill-after=2s; prlimit cpu 10:10, as 512 MiB, fsize 2 MiB, core 0;
- bwrap --unshare-all --die-with-parent --new-session;
- read-only /usr with /bin, /lib and /lib64 symlinks; /proc; minimal /dev;
- read-only /input holding only the pinned test (0444, directory 0555);
- read-only /packet holding the 20 known files, including the original MANIFEST.json and the routing file (the routing file is never read by the test);
- --clearenv, then PATH, LANG, PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE and MHASH_PACKET_DIR=/packet;
- python3 -I -B -v, with no home, no network, no writable mount and no subprocess;
- maximumAttempts 1.

The tool checker is read only as text. The test never touches any SDK, VM, prover, native crypto, private key or ledger. Notes (F7): -S is absent, so system .pth files under the read-only /usr still run; a bwrap or userns startup failure must count as STOP, not as RED.

RECOMMENDATION. I recommend authorising exactly one root-run ordinary RED under this exact envelope, once root has repeated its before-run checks (pins, modes, implementation absence, the F5 manifest precheck). This vote alone does not authorise it: a second fresh agreeing review and root's decision are still required.

Accept the RED only if all of the following hold:
- 'Ran 19 tests' and 'FAILED (failures=14)' with no errors;
- the 5 T00 methods report ok;
- each of the 14 failures ends with the exact M-HASH-RED phrase;
- exit code is 1;
- pins and modes are unchanged afterwards, and no new files exist.

Any other outcome means STOP with no retry. This vote grants nothing for implementation, GREEN, mutants, operator, builder or native work.
